<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/consult-laptop.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/consult-laptop-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/consult-laptop.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/nh-appraisal-services/">Services</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Neutral Umpire Appointments</span></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Insurance Appraisal Umpire Services in New Hampshire</h1><p class="lede">When two appraisers have narrowed a dispute as far as they can, a neutral umpire looks at what remains. We serve as umpire on New Hampshire panels with no ties to either side.</p><div class="btns"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line-light" href="/contact-nh-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><ul class="phero-chips"><li><small>Role</small><b>Neutral third member</b></li><li><small>Appointed by</small><b>Appraisers or court</b></li><li><small>Fees</small><b>Usually split</b></li></ul></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>The third chair on the panel</h2>
<p>Under most appraisal clauses, each side appoints its own appraiser, and those two choose an umpire. The umpire steps in only for the items the appraisers can’t agree on. A signed award by any two of the three sets the amount of loss.</p>
<p>In practice, this means the umpire is the tiebreaker. An appraiser who agrees with the umpire on a disputed item and signs the award settles it. That puts real weight on the umpire’s independence and competence.</p>
<h2>How umpires are chosen in New Hampshire</h2>
<p>Most policies say the two appraisers will select an umpire, often within 15 days. If they can’t agree, either side can ask a court of record in the state where the property is located to choose one. In New Hampshire, that is generally the Superior Court for the county — Hillsborough, Rockingham, Merrimack or wherever the property sits. Court appointment adds time and cost, so most panels work hard to agree.</p>
<p>A good umpire candidate has hands-on estimating or construction experience, understands how appraisal panels work, and has no business relationship with either appraiser that would raise eyebrows. Familiarity with the type of property helps too: a mill-building loss in Dover and a ski condo in Lincoln call for different expertise.</p>
<p>When we are proposed as umpire, we disclose any past dealings with the appraisers, carriers or policyholders involved before accepting.</p>
<h2>What a neutral umpire does</h2>
<ul>
<li>Reviews the policy’s appraisal clause and any agreed panel procedures.</li>
<li>Receives each appraiser’s estimate and the list of items still in dispute.</li>
<li>Inspects the property when needed, often with both appraisers present.</li>
<li>Considers measurements, photographs, pricing data and expert input from each side.</li>
<li>Decides each disputed item on the evidence and documents the reasoning.</li>
<li>Prepares or reviews an award form that clearly states replacement cost and actual cash value where applicable.</li>
</ul>
<h3>What an umpire won’t do</h3>
<p>An umpire doesn’t advocate for either side, doesn’t interpret coverage, and doesn’t decide questions of bad faith or claim handling. Those belong to the parties, the insurer, the New Hampshire Insurance Department or the courts. New Hampshire doesn’t have a broad first-party bad-faith penalty statute; remedies depend on the policy, contract law and the facts — ask an attorney.</p>
<h2>Independence and disclosure</h2>
<p>Because the umpire’s vote is often decisive, both sides need confidence in the umpire’s neutrality. We don’t serve as umpire on a claim where we have acted as an appraiser for either party, or where we have any financial interest in the outcome. If a potential conflict arises during the process, we disclose it.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Appraisers and umpire are different roles</b>New Hampshire Insurance Appraiser serves as a policyholder’s appointed appraiser on some claims and as a neutral umpire on others — never both on the same claim.</div>
<h2>Fees and scheduling</h2>
<p>Under most policy language, the umpire’s fees are shared equally by the policyholder and the insurer. Fees depend on the size of the claim, the number of disputed items and whether an inspection or hearing is needed. We agree on terms with both appraisers in writing before starting. We can schedule inspections across the state, from <a href="/new-hampshire-cities/portsmouth-nh/">Portsmouth</a> to <a href="/new-hampshire-cities/lebanon-hanover-nh/">Lebanon and Hanover</a>.</p>
<p>Appraisers looking for an umpire can reach us through the <a href="/contact-nh-appraiser/">contact page</a>. For a general overview of the process, see our <a href="/nh-appraisal-faq/">appraisal FAQ</a>.</p>

<h2>What owners ask about this</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Can a policyholder ask you to be the umpire?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>The umpire is normally selected by the two appraisers, not by either party directly. Either appraiser can propose us, and the other has to agree.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does the umpire have to hold a hearing?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Not necessarily. Many disputes are resolved on written submissions and an inspection. A more formal hearing may be used on larger or more complex claims, depending on what the panel agrees.</p></div></details><details><summary>What if the umpire’s decision seems wrong?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Appraisal awards are generally difficult to overturn, though courts may set one aside in limited circumstances such as fraud or a lack of impartiality. That is a legal question for a New Hampshire attorney.</p></div></details></div>
